  "subtitle": "Turn the room upside down",
  "summary": "Metal Storm is an NES action platformer built around the M-308 Gunner, a robot capable of reversing gravity. The player can transfer from floor to ceiling, changing which surface provides safety and which direction an obstacle must be approached from. Tamtex makes that ability the foundation of the entire game rather than a temporary novelty. Shooting remains important, but understanding a room’s two orientations is the essential skill. A corridor can contain another route simply because the player chooses to treat its ceiling as the ground.",
  "body": [
    "Metal Storm is an NES action platformer built around the M-308 Gunner, a robot capable of reversing gravity. The player can transfer from floor to ceiling, changing which surface provides safety and which direction an obstacle must be approached from. Tamtex makes that ability the foundation of the entire game rather than a temporary novelty. Shooting remains important, but understanding a room’s two orientations is the essential skill. A corridor can contain another route simply because the player chooses to treat its ceiling as the ground.",
    "Reversing gravity changes the robot’s position relative to projectiles, barriers and machinery. Some hazards behave differently according to its orientation, so a switch can solve one problem while exposing another. Later stages add screen-wrapping, allowing an exit from one edge to become an entrance from the opposite side. The player must keep track of those relationships while moving and firing. Metal Storm asks for spatial understanding through a very small number of actions, letting the level arrangement supply most of the complexity.",
    "The available power-ups include a stronger beam, a shield and protection during a gravity change, but only one can be held at a time. Movement and positioning consequently remain important even when a useful item has been collected. Completing the game unlocks an Expert Mode that rearranges enemies and hazards and changes boss patterns. Familiar rooms become new problems rather than merely faster repetitions. It is an extension of the original idea: understanding where the robot can stand matters as much as recognising the room it occupies."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Turn the room upside down",
    "One switch, several consequences",
    "Learning a second arrangement"
  ],
